*The imperial palace of China, located in the heart of Beijing.*

| Topic | Details |
|-------|---------|
| **Location** | 4 Chaoyangmen, Dongcheng District, Beijing, China (latitude 39.9163 N, longitude 116.3972 E). |
| **Historical Period** | Built 1406‑1420, served as the imperial palace for 24 emperors of the Ming (1368‑1644) and Qing (1644‑1912) dynasties. |
| **Size & Layout** | 72 m × 72 m (≈ 2.7 ha) of palace buildings, 980 m of walls, 8 km of roads, 999 rooms. The complex is divided into the Outer Court (political affairs) and Inner Court (royal family). |
| **Architectural Highlights** | • Hall of Supreme Harmony (largest hall, used for coronations).<br>• Hall of Central Harmony (state banquet hall).<br>• Hall of Imperial Ancestral Worship.<br>• Imperial Garden (the largest imperial garden in China).<br>• The iconic red walls, golden roofs, and sweeping upturned eaves. |
| **Cultural Significance** | • World Heritage Site (UNESCO, 1987).<br>• Symbol of Chinese imperial power and Confucian order.<br>• Home to the Palace Museum (博物馆), which houses over 1 million artifacts. |
| **Visiting Information** | • Opening Hours: 8:30 – 17:30 (last entry 16:30). Closed on Tuesdays, Chinese New Year, and the 1st Feb and 15th Feb (Qingming).<br>• Ticket Prices (2024):<br> – General admission: ¥60 (≈ $8).<br> – Free for children under 6, seniors over 60, and students with ID.<br> – Guided tours (English/Chinese): ¥120–¥200.<br>• Best Time to Visit: Early morning (first entry) or late afternoon to avoid crowds; spring (April–May) and autumn (September–October) offer mild weather.<br>• Tips: Wear comfortable shoes (the complex is large), bring a water bottle, and consider a 2‑hour guided tour to skip long lines. |
| **Nearby Attractions** | • Jingshan Park (hill behind the palace, great city view).<br>• Tiananmen Square (directly south, 1 km away).<br>• Beihai Park (historic lake and gardens).<br>• Wangfujing Street (shopping & street food). |
| **Transportation** | • Metro: Line 1 (Tiananmen East) or Line 2 (Tiananmen West).<br>• Bus: Multiple routes stop at Tiananmen Square. <br>• Taxi/ride‑share: 5–10 min from central Beijing. |
| **Cultural Etiquette** | • No photography inside the Hall of Supreme Harmony (unless in a guided tour).<br>• Respect the “no touching” rule for artifacts.<br>• Dress modestly; avoid hats and large bags. |
| **Fun Facts** | • The name “Forbidden City” comes from the “Imperial Palace” being off‑limits to the public. <br>• The palace complex contains 8 thousand rooms, but only 999 are counted because the number 9 is considered lucky. <br>• The palace’s roof tiles are made of glazed ceramic, giving the iconic golden hue. |

